```ts
function debounce<A extends unknown[]>(
  fn: (...args: A) => void,
  waitMs: number
): (...args: A) => void {
  let timer: ReturnType<typeof setTimeout> | undefined;
  return (...args) => {
    clearTimeout(timer);
    timer = setTimeout(() => fn(...args), waitMs);
  };
}
```

Every call resets the timer, so `fn` runs once, `waitMs` after the
last call. A search box uses this: no request per keystroke, one
request when typing pauses.

```ts
function throttle<A extends unknown[]>(
  fn: (...args: A) => void,
  intervalMs: number
): (...args: A) => void {
  let last = -Infinity;
  return (...args) => {
    const now = performance.now();
    if (now - last >= intervalMs) {
      last = now;
      fn(...args);
    }
  };
}
```

| | Debounce | Throttle |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Fires | after the stream goes quiet | during the stream, at a fixed rate |
| Guarantees | exactly one call per burst | at most one call per interval |
| Fits | search-as-you-type, autosave | scroll position, drag tracking |

<Callout kind="tip">
A scroll handler must not debounce: the page would only react after
scrolling stops. Pick by asking whether silence or cadence carries
the meaning.
</Callout>

<Callout kind="warn">
This throttle drops the trailing call: events landing inside the last
interval never fire. Track the final position separately, or add a
trailing timer, before using it for anything stateful.
</Callout>
